The bootloader updater.

A bootloader refuses USB writes below 0x4000, so it cannot replace itself.
This image is written to the application region like any firmware, and the
bootloader starts it. It carries the 16 KiB of bootloader region to install,
programs it through the part's own In-Application Programming commands, reads
it back, and resets into DFU so the next image can be written over it.

Build it with `make -C firmware bl_update`, which builds the bootloader first,
because the payload is that bootloader's binary. It is not in firmware/'s
`all` for the same reason the bootloader is not: it needs the ARM C++ runtime
to get there.

Install it with `ubertooth-dfu --update-bootloader -d bl_update.dfu`.
ubertooth-dfu(1), UPDATING THE BOOTLOADER is the user's procedure, including
what an interrupted write leaves and why a serial adapter has to be to hand.

Writing this image to a board is what programs the bootloader, so it programs
it whichever way the image got there: flashed as ordinary firmware, with no
`--update-bootloader`, it writes the region too, and the host's checks do not
run first.

The refusals are in ubertooth_interface.h and the write order in
../common/bl_update.h, which is where the ordering is explained; neither is
repeated here. This image is the part that needs the part, and
../tests/test_bl_update.c runs everything else on a host.
